</head>
<body>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ HEADER ═══ -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="label">Cross-Market Analytics Report &nbsp;·&nbsp; Short-Term Rental</div>
    <h1>Global Airbnb Market Comparison:<br/>Pricing, Supply &amp; Guest Demand Across 7 Major Cities</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      A data-forward cross-city analysis examining pricing dynamics, room-type composition, host concentration,
      and guest engagement patterns — calibrated for investment, market-entry, and regulatory benchmarking decisions.
    </p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <span><strong>Cities Covered:</strong> Amsterdam · Bangkok · Barcelona · London · Paris · Rome · Sydney</span>
      <span><strong>Total Listings Analysed:</strong> 50,000</span>
      <span><strong>Data Snapshot:</strong> 29 March 2026</span>
      <span><strong>Source:</strong> Inside Airbnb (CC BY 4.0)</span>
    </div>
    <nav class="toc">
      <a href="#market-overview">1 · Global Market Overview</a>
      <a href="#pricing-analysis">2 · Pricing Dynamics</a>
      <a href="#supply-structure">3 · Supply &amp; Host Concentration</a>
      <a href="#guest-demand">4 · Guest Demand &amp; Review Activity</a>
      <a href="#availability">5 · Availability &amp; Booking Constraints</a>
      <a href="#key-takeaways">6 · Key Takeaways &amp; Implications</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ DATA NOTE ═══ -->
<div class="container">
  <div style="padding: 24px 0 0;">
    <div class="data-note">
      <strong>Data Integrity Notes:</strong>
      The brief references 10 cities (including New York, Los Angeles, Tokyo); the available CSV extract covers 7 cities only — the three omitted cities are absent from the data and are not referenced in analysis.
      <strong>Paris and Sydney price data are entirely missing</strong> from this extract (0% coverage); their pricing cannot be computed and are excluded from all price charts and comparisons.
      <strong>Bangkok prices are listed as nominal values</strong> that are consistent with Thai Baht (median 1,400, min 200); the source brief states "local-equivalent USD" but these magnitudes strongly suggest THB — interpret Bangkok price rankings with this caveat.
      All other data is used as-is; no values have been imputed or fabricated.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 1 ═══ -->
<div class="container">
<section class="section" id="market-overview">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">01</span>
    <h2>Global Market Overview</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">50,000</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Listings in Dataset</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Across 7 major cities</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="kpi-value">16,674</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Largest Market</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">London — 3× larger than Rome</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card green">
      <div class="kpi-value">73.9%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Entire Home/Apt Share</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Cross-city average</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card red">
      <div class="kpi-value">51.8%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg Price Data Coverage</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Paris &amp; Sydney: 0% price data</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-grid">
    <div class="chart-panel">
      <div class="chart-title">Listing Count by City</div>
      <canvas id="listingsBarChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-panel">
      <div class="chart-title">Room Type Mix — All Cities Combined</div>
      <canvas id="roomTypePieChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    London dominates the dataset with <strong>16,674 listings</strong> — more than the combined count of Bangkok, Barcelona, and Amsterdam. Paris follows with 13,927, making the two European capitals the supply heavyweights. Rome (6,386), Bangkok (4,961), Barcelona (3,269), and Sydney (3,013) occupy a middle tier, while Amsterdam (1,770) is the smallest market represented but — as shown in later sections — among the most tightly regulated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Across all seven cities, <strong>Entire home/apt</strong> listings account for roughly 74% of supply, signalling that the short-term rental market globally skews toward whole-property commercialisation rather than room-sharing. Private rooms are the only meaningful secondary category (25%), while Hotel rooms (0.6%) and Shared rooms (0.3%) are marginal.
  </p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:32px; margin-bottom:16px;">Room Type Distribution by City</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>City</th>
          <th class="num">Total Listings</th>
          <th class="num">Entire Home %</th>
          <th class="num">Private Room %</th>
          <th class="num">Hotel Room %</th>
          <th class="num">Shared Room %</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="city-flag">Paris</td><td class="num">13,927</td><td class="num"><strong>88.2%</strong></td><td class="num">10.7%</td><td class="num">0.9%</td><td class="num">0.2%</td></tr>
