        <tr><td><strong>London</strong></td><td class="num">146.0</td><td class="num">102</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">5.5</td><td class="num">1.5%</td><td><span class="badge badge-low">Moderate</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Paris</strong></td><td class="num">125.2</td><td class="num">70</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">43.9</td><td class="num">12.6%</td><td><span class="badge badge-med">Regulated</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Amsterdam</strong></td><td class="num">92.5</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">4.1</td><td class="num">0.7%</td><td><span class="badge badge-high">Tightly Regulated</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:20px;">
    <strong>Bangkok's average 252.5 availability days per year</strong> (median: 295) is consistent with a market dominated by professional operators who maintain open calendars year-round. This is the highest in the dataset and supports the professional-host concentration findings in Section 3.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Amsterdam's low average availability (92.5 days; median 18)</strong> is the strongest regulatory fingerprint in the dataset. The city caps short-term rental at 30 nights per year for primary residences; the median of 18 days suggests most hosts are operating at well below the legal ceiling — or that listings are frequently near capacity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Barcelona's minimum-nights distribution is the most alarming indicator of regulatory arbitrage</strong>: 38.5% of listings require more than 30 nights — effectively operating as medium-term rentals to sidestep short-term rental licensing requirements. Barcelona has been restricting new STR permits since 2014; hosts with non-tourist licenses are converting to monthly-stay listings rather than exiting. Paris shows a similar but less extreme pattern (12.6%, mean minimum nights: 43.9 days).
  </p>

